// chords-demo.script — a comprehensive showcase of the chords package.
//
// The chords package draws guitar chord diagrams over the \picture layer. This
// demo exercises every feature of \chord: open and muted string markers, finger-
// number labels, plain (number-less) dots, barre chords via the optional
// [fret first last] argument, chords high up the neck that swap the nut for a
// "<n>fr" fret-window label, and the configuration variables that restyle the
// box. We frame it as a tiny chord book over the book format.

\use{book}
\use{chords}

\title{The Chord Box}
\author{A Tour of the chords Package}
\date{}

\frontmatter
\maketitle

\chapter{Preface}

This little book is a complete tour of the \texttt{chords} package. Every kind of
chord diagram the package can draw appears on the pages that follow: ordinary open
chords, chords with muted and open strings, dots that carry finger numbers and dots
that do not, barre chords drawn with a thick bar, and shapes high up the neck where
the nut gives way to a fret-position label. The final chapter shows how the diagram
can be restyled.

\mainmatter

\chapter{Reading a Chord Box}

A chord diagram is a picture of the top of the neck as you look down at it. The six
vertical lines are the strings, from the lowest and thickest on the left to the
highest and thinnest on the right. The horizontal lines are the frets, and the thick
line across the top is the nut. A black dot shows where to press a string, and the
number inside it names the finger: \textbf{1} index, \textbf{2} middle, \textbf{3}
ring, \textbf{4} little finger. Above the nut an ``o'' marks an open string and an
``×'' marks a string you do not play.

\bigskip
\centerline{\chord{C}{x 3 2 0 1 0}{. 3 2 . 1 .}}
\bigskip

This is C major. The lowest string carries an ``×'' (do not play it), the fourth and
top strings ring open under their ``o'' marks, and three numbered dots show where the
ring, middle and index fingers press.

\chapter{Open Chords}

Open chords mix fretted dots with open and muted strings, and they sit at the top of
the neck so each one is drawn from the nut. Here is the family every beginner learns
first, each with its fingers numbered.

\bigskip
\centerline{
  \chord{Em}{0 2 2 0 0 0}{. 2 3 . . .}\hskip 24
  \chord{E}{0 2 2 1 0 0}{. 2 3 1 . .}\hskip 24
  \chord{Am}{x 0 2 2 1 0}{. . 2 3 1 .}
}
\bigskip
\centerline{
  \chord{A}{x 0 2 2 2 0}{. . 1 2 3 .}\hskip 24
  \chord{D}{x x 0 2 3 2}{. . . 1 3 2}\hskip 24
  \chord{G}{3 2 0 0 0 3}{2 1 . . . 3}
}
\bigskip

E minor and E major leave several strings open. A minor, A major and D major each
mute the lowest one or two strings, so their diagrams open with ``×'' marks. G major
spreads three fingers wide across the neck while leaving the middle three strings
open.

\chapter{Plain Dots Without Numbers}

Passing an empty finger list draws plain dots with no numbers inside — useful for a
chart that only shows shapes, or once the fingering is second nature. Here are the
same C and G chords with bare dots.

\bigskip
\centerline{
  \chord{C}{x 3 2 0 1 0}{}\hskip 30
  \chord{G}{3 2 0 0 0 3}{}
}
\bigskip

The open and muted markers above the nut are unchanged; only the numbers inside the
dots are gone.

\chapter{Barre Chords}

A barre is one finger laid flat across several strings. It is given with the optional
bracket argument \texttt{[fret first last]}: the fret it lies on, and the first and
last string columns it covers, numbered 1 (low E, far left) to 6 (high e, far right).
The package draws it as a thick round bar, and the dot that would land on the barre's
own fret is left out, since the bar already covers it.

\section{F Major}

F major lays the index finger flat across all six strings at the first fret, written
\texttt{[1 1 6]}, with the rest of the E-major shape stacked on top.

\bigskip
\centerline{\chord[1 1 6]{F}{1 3 3 2 1 1}{1 3 4 2 1 1}}
\bigskip

\section{B Major}

B major barres five strings at the second fret, \texttt{[2 2 6]}, muting the lowest
string. Its bar starts at column 2, so the low E keeps its ``×'' above the nut.

\bigskip
\centerline{\chord[2 2 6]{B}{x 2 4 4 4 2}{. 1 3 4 2 1}}
\bigskip

\chapter{High Up the Neck}

When the fretted notes do not fit in the first four frets, the diagram shifts up the
neck. The nut is replaced by an ordinary fret line, and a small ``\,fr'' label to the
right of the top row says which fret the window starts on. The barre form works just
the same up here.

\section{A Movable Barre Shape}

The F shape slid up the neck becomes B-flat at the first fret and other chords higher
up. Here it is at the fifth fret (A) and the seventh (B), each labelled with its starting
fret. Notice there is no nut and no open strings — every string is fretted by the
barre or a finger above it.

\bigskip
\centerline{
  \chord[5 1 6]{A}{5 7 7 6 5 5}{1 3 4 2 1 1}\hskip 30
  \chord[7 1 6]{B}{7 9 9 8 7 7}{1 3 4 2 1 1}
}
\bigskip

\section{A Closed Voicing}

A chord can sit entirely within a window further up the neck without a barre at all.
This G shape at the tenth fret has its highest note at the twelfth, so it is drawn in
a window starting at the tenth fret.

\bigskip
\centerline{\chord{G}{3 x 0 0 0 3}{2 . . . . 3}}
\bigskip
\centerline{\chord{D}{x x 12 11 10 10}{. . 4 3 1 1}}
\bigskip

The first diagram is the open G again, for comparison; the second is a closed D
voicing up the neck, drawn in its ``10\,fr'' window.

\chapter{Restyling the Box}

Every dimension and colour of the diagram is a configuration variable you can set
after \texttt{\\use\{chords\}}. Sizes are in points. Here the same C chord is drawn
larger and in a different ink by overriding a few of them.

\bigskip
\set chordcw {18}
\set chordrh {21}
\set chorddot {6}
\set chordcolor {navy}
\set chordnamesize {17}
\centerline{\chord{C}{x 3 2 0 1 0}{. 3 2 . 1 .}}
\bigskip

The grid is wider and taller, the dots are bigger, the name is set in a larger size,
and the whole diagram is drawn in navy. Resetting the variables restores the default
look.

\bigskip
\set chordcw {13}
\set chordrh {15}
\set chorddot {4.4}
\set chordcolor {black}
\set chordnamesize {13}
\centerline{\chord{C}{x 3 2 0 1 0}{. 3 2 . 1 .}}
\bigskip

\backmatter

\chapter{Summary of Features}

This tour covered open chords with mixed open and muted strings, finger-number labels
and plain number-less dots, barre chords via the \texttt{[fret first last]} argument,
movable shapes high up the neck with the ``\,fr'' window label in place of the nut,
and the configuration variables that restyle the box.
